    <h2>The Bugs You See Aren't the Problem</h2>

    <p>The pests crossing the kitchen floor are the symptom. The problem is that Cullman's long, humid warm season keeps insect populations breeding most of the year, and the county's geography feeds it — wooded lots, pasture and row-crop land, and homes tucked against tree lines all sit inside that pressure. The bugs you see are a fraction of the ones you don't, in the wall voids, the crawlspace, and along the woods edge if your lot backs up to timber, as a lot of properties out toward Holly Pond and Baileyton do. Kill the visible ones and the reservoir behind the walls simply repopulates.</p>

    <h2>Why One Spray Never Holds</h2>

    <p>A single treatment does two things and misses the one that matters. It kills active insects and leaves a residual good for a few weeks. What it doesn't do is change how pests enter — the 3/8-inch gap under a door, the hole where a pipe or dryer vent passes the wall, the crawlspace vent with no screen. What goes wrong is predictable: the homeowner pays for the spray, enjoys a clean few weeks, and reads the pests' return as a bad job, when a one-time spray was never built to hold. It treated the symptom and left the door open.</p>

    <h2>The Part People Underrate: Termites</h2>

    <p>General pests are a nuisance. Subterranean termites are a structural threat, and they're the reason a Cullman homeowner should think maintenance, not emergency. Termite pressure is heavy this far south, and the older homes near downtown — the German-heritage stock where framing sits closer to grade and decades of moisture have softened things — give colonies more avenues in. The practitioner detail worth knowing: a spring swarm of winged termites inside the house isn't an early warning, it's proof of an established, mature colony already working. Swarm season runs March through May. The people who never have a termite disaster are the ones carrying an annual bond that monitors and treats before the damage, not after.</p>

    <h2>What Actually Keeps Pressure Down</h2>

    <p>Effective control is two disciplines the cheap version skips. First, exclusion: physically sealing the entry points so the house stops being easy to get into — the part that actually reduces the problem instead of resetting it. Second, a recurring schedule, usually quarterly, that maintains a treated barrier through the seasons and catches new pressure early. For termites, a liquid barrier plus an annual bond keeps the structure protected and watched. Landscaping choices matter too — firewood stacked against the house and mulch piled to the siding are termite highways, which is why pest control and a <a href="/locations/cullman/landscaper/">landscaper</a> end up connected out here. The honest admission: none of this makes a house permanently pest-free. It manages an ongoing pressure down to where you rarely see it, which is the real, achievable goal — and the small stuff a <a href="/locations/cullman/handyman/">handyman</a> seals up between visits helps hold the line.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Pest Control Costs in Cullman</h2>

    <p>Real ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Quarterly general pest service</strong> — about $40 to $60 a visit, with a higher first visit ($125 to $175).</li>
      <li><strong>Termite liquid barrier treatment</strong> — roughly $8 to $15 per linear foot of foundation.</li>
      <li><strong>Annual termite bond</strong> — about $150 to $300 a year, cheap insurance against structural repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Exclusion / sealing</strong> — priced by scope; the step that turns a recurring problem into a rare one.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The math that matters: a termite bond costs a few hundred dollars a year, and termite structural repairs run into the thousands. Skipping the bond to save the fee is the expensive choice — it just doesn't feel like it until the damage is found.</p>

    <h2>Getting Ahead of Swarm Season</h2>

    <p>Beat termite swarm season — a spring inspection and an active bond before March beats a call after you find wings on the windowsill. For general pests, spring and early fall are natural reset points as populations shift. If you're seeing winged insects indoors, don't wait to decide whether they're termites or flying ants; a real inspection settles it in one visit, and the difference is a nuisance versus a structural bill.</p>

    <h2>Common Pest Questions in Cullman County</h2>

    <p><strong>Can I just spray once?</strong></p>
    <p>For a wasp nest, yes. For general pests, no — it fades in weeks and ignores how they get in. Recurring service plus sealing is the honest model.</p>

    <p><strong>Do I need termite protection?</strong></p>
    <p>Yes. Termite pressure is heavy here and the older downtown homes are more exposed. An annual bond for a few hundred dollars beats thousands in repairs.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Quarterly service $40 to $60, termite treatment $8 to $15 a linear foot, an annual bond $150 to $300.</p>

